# Consolidated size-target guides.
#
# Five near-identical pages once covered "compress to 50 KB", "to 100 KB", "to
# 500 KB" and "to 1 MB", all carrying the same benchmark table under a
# different number. Google indexed exactly one of them and left the rest as
# "URL is unknown to Google" or "Discovered - currently not indexed", which is
# the duplicate filter doing its job. The survivor now answers every one of
# those targets in one table, and the other four point at it.
/blog/compress-pdf-to-100kb-free-offline                       /blog/compress-pdf-to-100kb-free-offline-without-losing   301!
/blog/compress-pdf-to-1mb-free-online-offline                  /blog/compress-pdf-to-100kb-free-offline-without-losing   301!
/blog/compress-pdf-to-50kb-offline-online-free                 /blog/compress-pdf-to-100kb-free-offline-without-losing   301!
/blog/compress-pdf-to-500kb-online-free-offline-without-losing /blog/compress-pdf-to-100kb-free-offline-without-losing   301!

# SPA fallback.
#
# Every real route is prerendered to its own index.html by scripts/prerender.mjs,
# so Netlify serves those as static files before this rule is ever consulted.
# What reaches this line is a URL that does not exist, which is why it returns
# 404 rather than 200: a 200 here would make Google index every typo as a
# duplicate of the homepage. The app shell still loads and renders its own
# not-found page.
/*    /index.html   404
